    <h1>Do Facebook Ads Actually Work <span class="amber">For Tradesmen?</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">Yes, Facebook ads work for tradesmen, but only when they run to a proper qualifying funnel instead of a basic website. The ad gets the attention; the funnel does the selling. Most trades who swear ads don't work never had the funnel in the first place.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="updated">Written by <a href="/about/">Kaine Cruickshanks</a>, ex-tradesman and founder of CopyByKaine &middot; Last updated September 2026</p>

      <p>I hear it constantly: "I tried Facebook ads, waste of money, they don't work for my trade." Nearly every time, the same thing had gone wrong. The ad wasn't the problem. Everything that happened after the click was.</p>

      <h2>Why most trades' Facebook ads fail</h2>
      <p>A typical trade sets up an ad, boosts a post, and sends anyone who clicks to their Facebook page or a basic website. There's no clear next step, no question to answer, nothing that separates a serious homeowner from someone killing time on their phone. So the only "leads" that trickle in are the tyre-kickers, the ones after a cheap price and nothing else. The good homeowners click, see nothing that grabs them, and carry on scrolling.</p>
      <p>Then the verdict lands: ads don't work. But the ad did its job. It got the click. What was missing was everything designed to turn that click into a booked job.</p>

      <h2>What actually makes them work</h2>
      <p>Three things, and you need all three:</p>
      <h3>1. A qualifying funnel, not a website</h3>
      <p>The click has to land on a page with one job: sort the serious from the browsers. It asks the homeowner what the job is, where they are, when they want it done and how to reach them. A tyre-kicker can't be bothered. A homeowner ready to spend answers it in under a minute. By the time the lead reaches your phone, it's already been filtered.</p>
      <h3>2. Real footage of your work</h3>
      <p>Homeowners can smell a stock advert a mile off. Real footage of your actual jobs, the finish, the before-and-after, stops the scroll because it looks real. It builds trust before they've even messaged. This is the bit most agencies skip, and it's the bit that makes the difference.</p>
      <h3>3. Fast follow-up</h3>
      <p>The trade that replies first usually wins the job. So the lead has to hit your phone the second it comes in, with automated follow-up behind it so nothing goes cold while you're up a ladder. Speed is half the battle.</p>

      <h2>What results actually look like</h2>
      <p>Realistically, once the ads and funnel are live, leads usually start coming through in the first week or two. The early stretch is about learning which angle and audience pull best for your trade, then leaning into what works. It's not a magic tap you turn on for instant jobs, it's a system that builds. But a proper setup gives you a steady stream of homeowner enquiries you can actually plan a business around, instead of waiting on word of mouth.</p>

      <h2>Which trades does it work best for?</h2>
      <p>Any trade that gets its work from homeowners. I run ads for <a href="/industries/exterior-cleaning/">exterior cleaners</a>, <a href="/industries/media-walls/">media wall installers</a>, <a href="/industries/fitted-wardrobes/">fitted wardrobe companies</a>, <a href="/industries/windows-doors/">window and door fitters</a>, <a href="/industries/joiners/">joiners</a>, <a href="/industries/kitchens/">kitchen</a> and <a href="/industries/bathrooms/">bathroom fitters</a> and more. If homeowners pay your invoices and you can handle more work, it can work for you. If you only do trade-to-trade or commercial contracts, it's a different game.</p>

      <h2>So, is it worth it?</h2>
      <p>If you've got the capacity to take on more work and you can get back to enquiries quickly, then yes. The trades that get burned are the ones running ads with no funnel, or the ones who can't reply for three days. Get the system right and reply fast, and Facebook ads are one of the most reliable ways for a tradesman to keep the diary full.</p>

